{"agent_id":"pp3g-MAMp-quick-pass-plan-review-of-341-read-only-hy3-read-7d00","agent_seq":4,"timestamp":"2026-08-10T01:19:58.098278404Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"be776be6-96c3-4686-8772-ececc379dacf","comment_uuid":"5c673ba8-b153-406a-a1f6-8a7f859427c4","author":"pp3g-MAMp-quick-pass-plan-review-of-341-read-only-hy3-read-7d00","content":"=== hy3 QUICK-PASS PLAN REVIEW — VERDICT: MAKE CHANGES ===\n\nRead-only sanity pass on the #341 plan. Plan is directionally correct and its core premise (a real, prepared-but-never-filed canonical set + an empty ASES methodology layer) is VERIFIED. It is not safe to execute as written: one classification is factually inverted, one acceptance criterion is unsatisfiable, and Step 3 rests on an attribution that contradicts two canonical documents. All are fixable in the plan text; none require abandoning it.\n\n--- Q1. Does the classification match the actual files? PARTLY. ---\n\nCATEGORY A (identical) — CORRECT, 5/5 verified byte-identical:\n  canonical-terminology / concept-levels-of-abstraction / documenation-standards /\n  documentation-taxonomy / ai-capability-registry-spec. No re-file needed. Confirmed.\n\nCATEGORY C (no committed counterpart) — CORRECT as a set. Verified against git ls-files docs/*:\n  none of ai-orchestration-guide, methodology-to-requirements-mapping-spec,\n  execution-engine-vision, SKILL-clean-room-execution, edases-methodology-review,\n  repo-review-checklist, proposed-implementation-layer exist as committed docs.\n  Corroborated by git show --stat e082d65c: that commit filed 5 standards +\n  frameworks/protocols/registry/syntheses and NOT these 7. Finding 1 and Finding 2 hold.\n\nCATEGORY B — HALF WRONG. This is the blocking defect.\n\n  (B1) evaluation-framework — plan is RIGHT that staged has frontmatter and committed\n  does not, but WRONG to call it a major/structured diff. Staged is 367 lines, committed\n  338, delta 29 = exactly the YAML block, plus one lost blank line before the final\n  paragraph of Relationship to Process. Body is otherwise identical. This is a mechanical\n  re-add of frontmatter, not judgement work.\n\n  (B2) ai-evaluation-protocol — RIGHT and trivial: an 11-item bullet list reordered. Nothing else.\n\n  (B3) to-file/project-update/top-level/ — CLASSIFICATION IS INVERTED. MUST FIX.\n  The plan states: 'staged adds frontmatter + restructure'. The opposite is true.\n  Evidence:\n    - Committed README.md / ORIENTATION.md / ARCHITECTURE.md / AGENTS.md each ALREADY carry\n      the YAML block, including supersedes: <NAME>.md (previous version).\n    - The staged top-level files carry NO frontmatter at all.\n    - Diff of git show e082d65c:ORIENTATION.md against the staged copy = the 10-line\n      frontmatter plus ONE sentence (staged: 'Translates methodological principles into\n      implementation requirements'; committed: 'Translates methodological rules into\n      capabilities that software must provide'). Nothing else differs.\n    - Diff of git show e082d65c:AGENTS.md against the staged copy = the 10-line frontmatter\n      and nothing else.\n  So the staged top-level set is the PRE-FILING DRAFT that e082d65c superseded. It is\n  Category A-equivalent (superseded staging, safe to drop), not Category B.\n\n  Consequence if unfixed: Step 2 instructs 'prefer staged-with-frontmatter where it is a\n  superset'. Applied to top-level/ that rule inverts and REVERTS live governance docs to\n  2026-06-29 drafts, destroying the sections HEAD AGENTS.md has gained since — Reasoning\n  Certainty, Cheapest-Test-First, Workflow Topology, Model Discipline, Research Programme —\n  i.e. currently-binding agent operating rules. The 'operator decision / do not delete'\n  guardrail limits the blast radius but the operator would be deciding on a false premise.\n\n--- Q2. Are the target homes correct per each doc's frontmatter layer? YES, with 4 caveats. ---\n\n  Verified frontmatter:\n    ai-orchestration-guide            -> programme ASES,   layer Methodology  -> docs/methodology/  CORRECT\n    methodology-to-requirements-spec  -> programme ASES,   layer Requirements -> docs/requirements/ CORRECT\n    execution-engine-vision           -> program Tooling,  layer Architecture -> docs/architecture/ CORRECT\n  Layer names match the permitted set in Documentation Standard and the\n  Research -> Methodology -> Requirements -> Architecture -> Implementation chain in\n  ORIENTATION and Documentation Taxonomy. Adding these three dirs alongside the existing\n  docs/research/ and docs/standards/ is coherent.\n\n  Caveat 2a — execution-engine-vision declares canonical_repository: ases-engine, not edases.\n  Filing it into this repo needs an explicit note that this is a mirror/placeholder, otherwise\n  it silently contradicts Documentation Standard 'canonical_repository identifies the\n  authoritative repository'.\n  Caveat 2b — field-name drift. Documentation Standard mandates the key 'program'.\n  ai-orchestration-guide and methodology-to-requirements-mapping-spec use 'programme'.\n  Step 1 should normalise this, and Documentation Standard itself is internally inconsistent\n  (example block says last_update, definitions say last_updated).\n  Caveat 2c — SKILL-clean-room-execution has MALFORMED frontmatter: the YAML block is never\n  closed, depends_on/consumed_by use markdown asterisk bullets rather than YAML sequences, and\n  last_updated has been swallowed into a markdown heading. Same defect in\n  edases-methodology-review and repo-review-checklist. Step 1 must repair, not just relocate.\n  Caveat 2d — UNRESOLVED CONFLICT the plan does not flag: proposed-implementation-layer.md,\n  sitting in the same staging dir, is a decision record arguing that the Clean Room Execution\n  Guide is NOT methodology but an implementation of methodology and 'should move out of the\n  canonical documentation entirely' into a tooling layer. That directly contradicts routing it\n  to docs/methodology/. Step 1 must resolve this before moving that file, not after.\n\n--- Q3. Is the re-attribution to the methodology layer sound? NO, not as stated. MUST FIX. ---\n\n  Counts verified: harness-evaluations 5, capability-mapping 2, selection-rationale 3,\n  core-system-prompts 2. Good.\n  But NONE of these 12 files has any frontmatter. Step 3 says 'update layer metadata' — it is\n  actually AUTHORING metadata for 12 files from scratch. Different size and risk of task.\n\n  3a. harness-evaluations -> Methodology is UNSOUND. These are OBS/FIN evidence documents.\n  The template mandates 'Observations — keep these atomic and objective, avoid interpretation'\n  and 'Findings — conclusions derived from observations', with verification-status tags\n  (verified-directly / per-subagent / inferred-from-code). This structure is MACHINE-ENFORCED:\n  scripts/validate_evaluation.py REQUIRED_SECTIONS demands sections 1-6 including Observations\n  and Findings. Documentation Taxonomy defines exactly this class as Research Records\n  ('preserve observations, experiments and findings; evidence-driven; INPUTS TO methodology\n  development'). AGENTS.md states plainly: 'Evidence should not be presented as methodology.'\n  Reclassifying these to Methodology breaks the plan's own carve-out rule (it keeps\n  observations/ and findings/ in research) and violates a canonical rule. Keep as Research.\n  Note the same logic that keeps AI Capability Registry in research applies here: the AI\n  Orchestration Guide itself says it is normative 'unlike the AI Capability Registry', which is\n  descriptive. harness-evaluations are descriptive capability evidence — same class.\n\n  3b. selection-rationale -> UNSOUND as Methodology. These are ADRs about which harness to\n  EVALUATE NEXT (Track B, Crosslink issues 3 and 4), with 'Evidence expected to validate' and\n  falsification clauses. They are research-process decision records. document_type: ADR,\n  layer: Research.\n\n  3c. capability-mapping -> SPLIT, not one bucket. Harness-Capability-Matrix is a coverage\n  matrix derived from research-addenda/Research Addendum 01 with cells citing\n  harness-evaluations — a derived research artefact. Model-Routing-Matrix is operational\n  routing config consumed by SESSION-START.md at dispatch time — Implementation/Tooling.\n  Treating both as 'methodology -> requirements' conflates them.\n\n  3d. core-system-prompts -> the most defensible of the four, but still contested by this\n  repo's own reasoning: proposed-implementation-layer.md argues normative agent artefacts are\n  implementations of methodology and belong in tooling. The plan should argue this explicitly\n  rather than assert Methodology.\n\n  3e. TERMINOLOGY DEFECT: 'methodology-support' and 'decision records' are used as if they were\n  layer values. Documentation Standard permits exactly five: Research, Methodology,\n  Requirements, Architecture, Implementation. The plan is conflating layer with document_type.\n\n  3f. The three _template.md files must be excluded from canonical classification — a template\n  is not an artefact with a layer.\n\n  3g. The move itself is questionable on canonical grounds. Documentation Standard, Repository\n  Independence: 'Repository location should not determine document identity... repositories may\n  therefore be reorganised without changing document meaning.' ORIENTATION repeats\n  'documentation should expose relationships explicitly rather than relying upon directory\n  structure.' The metadata correction is cheap, reversible and defensible on its own; the\n  git mv is expensive, breakage-prone and NOT required by the standard. Separate them.\n\n--- Q4. Is the 4-step sequencing right? YES. Keep it. ---\n\n  Filing the missing layer BEFORE re-attributing is correct, for a reason worth recording:\n  Step 3 needs a destination whose SEMANTICS are already anchored. docs/methodology/ only\n  means something once the AI Orchestration Guide (the normative methodology spec) is in it.\n  Re-attributing first would be moving files into an empty, undefined container. The ordering\n  also happens to be correct risk-first: Step 1 is low-risk high-value, Step 2 is judgement,\n  Step 3 is high-churn. Two refinements:\n    4a. Step 3 already contains an inline sweep and Step 4 repeats it. A trailing batch sweep\n        leaves a window of broken references between commits. Fold the sweep into EACH move\n        commit; keep Step 4 as navigation/ORIENTATION/ARCHITECTURE update only.\n    4b. Step 3 is 12 files + frontmatter authoring + roughly 80 references across 27 files.\n        That is not one commit and arguably not this issue. Split it out.\n\n--- Q5. Showstoppers? No absolute FAIL. Two near-showstoppers, both fixable in the plan. ---\n\n  5a. The inverted top-level/ classification (B3). Highest severity: acting on it regresses\n      live governance docs.\n  5b. ACCEPTANCE CRITERION 2 IS UNSATISFIABLE AS WRITTEN. 'Every ORIENTATION-claimed canonical\n      doc resolves to a committed file' — ORIENTATION lists NINE canonical docs. Step 1 fixes\n      three of them. The fourth gap is 'AI Capability Registry': only\n      docs/research/registry/AI Capability Registry Specification.md exists. The Specification\n      is not the Registry — the spec itself distinguishes them. No registry instance exists in\n      the committed tree OR in the staged set, so no amount of Step 1 work satisfies AC2.\n      Either scope the registry instance out explicitly, or restate AC2 as\n      'resolves to a committed file or a recorded, issue-tracked gap'.\n\n  Mechanism check — CLEAR, no blocker. All 18 staged files under to-file/project-update are\n  git-tracked (added at 3e7eac9b, 2026-07-11, i.e. 12 days AFTER the superseding commit\n  e082d65c on 2026-06-29 — worth noting: the staging dir was committed to git after it was\n  already stale). git mv will work. Working tree is clean.\n  Also clear: scripts/validate_evaluation.py detect_doc_type matches on path PARTS, so moving\n  harness-evaluations/ survives as long as the directory NAME is preserved. But the plan's\n  sweep scope (CHANGELOG, .design, research-addenda, knowledge pages) OMITS scripts/ — add it.\n\n--- REQUIRED CHANGES BEFORE EXECUTION ---\n  R1. Reclassify to-file/project-update/top-level/ from Category B to superseded staging;\n      strike 'staged adds frontmatter + restructure'; strike the 'prefer staged' rule for it.\n  R2. Downgrade B1/B2 from 'judgement work' to mechanical: B1 = re-add YAML frontmatter to the\n      committed Evaluation Framework; B2 = pick a bullet order. Record which and move on.\n  R3. Restate AC2 to account for the AI Capability Registry instance gap.\n  R4. Rewrite Step 3: keep harness-evaluations and selection-rationale in Research; split\n      capability-mapping (matrix = Research, routing matrix = Implementation/Tooling); argue\n      core-system-prompts explicitly; drop the non-existent 'methodology-support' layer;\n      exclude _template.md; and SPLIT the metadata correction from the git mv, doing metadata\n      first and treating relocation as a separate operator-approved decision given\n      Documentation Standard Repository Independence.\n  R5. Step 1 must repair malformed YAML (SKILL-clean-room-execution, edases-methodology-review,\n      repo-review-checklist), normalise programme -> program, and resolve the\n      proposed-implementation-layer.md contradiction before routing the clean-room guide.\n  R6. Add scripts/ to the reference-sweep scope; fold the sweep into each move commit.\n\nSTEP 1 AS SCOPED (minus R5 repairs) IS SAFE TO PROCEED. Steps 2 and 3 are not.\n\n--- REASONING CERTAINTY ---\nWHY: the plan's premises were checked against the working tree and git history rather than\n  re-read from the issue text.\nWHAT: byte-comparison of all 5 Category A pairs; frontmatter reads of all Category C files;\n  content diffs of all 3 Category B groups; diffs of staged top-level against BOTH e082d65c\n  and e082d65c^; git show --stat of e082d65c and 3e7eac9b; git ls-files docs/*;\n  git grep counts of the four re-attribution targets; reads of Documentation Standard,\n  Documentation Taxonomy, ORIENTATION, AGENTS.md, scripts/validate_evaluation.py.\nHOW CERTAIN: PROVEN for Q1 (A, C, and the B3 inversion), Q2 layer mapping, Q5 mechanism, and\n  the AC2 gap — all direct file/git evidence. EVIDENCE-BASED for Q3 (rests on canonical text\n  in Documentation Taxonomy plus AGENTS.md 'evidence is not methodology', plus the machine-\n  enforced OBS/FIN structure). JUDGEMENT for Q4 sequencing and for 3d core-system-prompts.\nWHAT-NOT-TESTED: I did not diff the bodies of the 7 Category C files against any historical\n  version, so I cannot say whether they are current. I did not read the full text of\n  README/ARCHITECTURE staged-vs-committed beyond their frontmatter headers (I extrapolated the\n  supersession direction from ORIENTATION and AGENTS, which were proven). I did not verify\n  content of research-addenda/Research Addendum 01. I did not execute\n  scripts/validate_evaluation.py. I did not assess whether the ~80 references would in fact all\n  resolve after a move — only that scripts/ was omitted from the stated sweep scope. I read no\n  issue other than #341.","created_at":"2026-08-10T01:19:58.090336308Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:gmAarmjFCEvwz+CgOEYPLcIOhhzmWXabB+SI/0GQSws","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgurGi2jDobuotc4RlWYVlHd3WLQ5lrTaeZJG3PjFMApYAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAF7Ju/BD8Hui5f2f8bmPGrUUX5Zf5fF2np77mFE6J+FkhrUuP8Suy4Yo3rlJ/mILhLptrqIPcEKcdrW3bDOjLCQ=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:gmAarmjFCEvwz+CgOEYPLcIOhhzmWXabB+SI/0GQSws","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgurGi2jDobuotc4RlWYVlHd3WLQ5lrTaeZJG3PjFMApYAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQGpCTot+44m+PmgucrO+oBNoBfuGqYvq7uN3TKE75HeI/0lfH9heqKLhUcrS521TDjPxtBi0MUaP1dS62+ic3AI="}
